{
  "dom_id": "step-q72748",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Suppose $B$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $det(B)=-7.$ The matrix $C$ is obtained from $B$ by applying the elementary row operations ${R}_{2}:=2{R}_{2}$ and ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}.$ What is the value of $det(C)?$",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}$ involves swapping two rows, so it changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{2}:=2{R}_{2}$ involves multiplying a row by $2,$ so the determinant is multiplied by $2$ as well.\n\nTherefore, we have to change the sign of the determinant to the opposite one and multiply by $2$ as well. Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(C) & =2\\cdot (-det(B)) \\\\  & =-2det(B) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot (-7) \\\\  & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
